From 8f54a8660fb1bff886499b8ffaa661e0828cd2b4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: yekai pan <pyk@seas.upenn.edu>
Date: Sat, 3 Oct 2026 00:34:49 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] l2r-local: bug 11: run SCCP across calls only within a budget
 of call sites

The pipeline runs MLIR's `sccp` twice on the whole module, which makes it
interprocedural, and its cost is quadratic in the call sites of a
callable: each time the analysis finds one more call site of a function,
or the arguments at one change, MLIR's data-flow framework visits the
function again and walks all its known call sites
(`DeadCodeAnalysis::visitCallableTerminator` adds the function's returns
as predecessors of each call, `AbstractSparseForwardDataFlowAnalysis::
visitCallableOperation` joins each call's arguments into the function's).
One function called from 4000 places took 7 s in `sccp` alone; a lean2rr
program using Std.Http (17,197 functions) was still in the first `sccp`
after 50 minutes.

Add `reussir-sccp`, which the C API's SCCP factory now returns: it counts
the call sites of each callee and runs the stock `sccp` on the module when
the sum over callees of the square of their call sites is at most
`max-call-site-pairs` (2^22), and nested on each `func.func` otherwise
(stock SCCP on one function treats arguments and call results as
unknown; LLVM's IPSCCP still propagates across calls later). Only callees
with a body count: the analysis treats a declaration (an FFI import, a
runtime function) as external and never walks its call sites (review
RV8C-04). Programs under the budget are compiled exactly as before:
lean2rr's classic corpus has at most 1.5M pairs.

reussir-bugs/repros/bug11-sccp-call-graph.py (rrc -O aggressive, to an
executable, loaded machine): N = 2000 (4.0M pairs, still across calls):
2.9-3.2 s before, 2.8-3.9 s after; N = 4000 (16M pairs): 8.9 s -> 4.2 s.
The Std.Http program (335M pairs) gets SCCP per function: 3 s and 19 s
for the two runs.

Test: tests/integration/conversion/sccp_call_site_budget.mlir.
